[include version_include.man]

[manpage_begin ns_accesslog n [vset version]]
[moddesc   {NaviServer Module Commands}]
[titledesc {Query and control access log parameters}]

[description]

This command controls and configures the access log of the server.
The access log records all served requests of a server containing
timestamp, IP address of the requester, the queried resource, the
HTTP result code, the transmitted amount of data, etc. In general, it
follows the more or less standardized access log file format (NCSA
format, common log format) but can be influence to contain more
information, useful for log file analysis, debugging and statistics.

[para] In most cases, the access log is controlled via the parameter
specified in the configuration file (see below). For example, when the
server is running in reverse proxy mode, the value provided by the
reverse proxy server via request header fields will be reported in the
log file instead of the IP address of the physically connected host.
Several parameters can be queried and altered as well at run-time via
the command [cmd "ns_accesslog"].



[section {COMMANDS}]
[list_begin definitions]


[call [cmd "ns_accesslog extendedheaders"] \
	[opt [arg headers]]]

Return a list of the HTTP header fields which will be appended to each entry in the
access log. If [arg headers] is given it replaces the existing
setting.

[para] By default, all specified header fields are request header
fields (like e.g., {Referer x-forwarded-for}). Optionally one can
specify the request header fields with a prefix "request:" or "response"
to denote request or response header fields. The prefix can be
abbreviated to 3 characters (example: {req:Host response:content-type}).
Note that always the request header fields are reported before the
response header fields. Caveat: certain response header fields (e.g., "Date"
or "Server" are not accessible this way).


[call [cmd "ns_accesslog file"] \
	[opt [arg filepath]] ]

Sets or gets the [arg filepath] of the access log. When [arg filepath]
is not provided, the command teturns the path of the access log for
the current virtual server.

[para]
If [arg filepath] is given then the old log is closed and the log
re-opened at the new location. All the components of [arg filepath] must
exist. If an error occurs logging will be disabled.


[call [cmd "ns_accesslog flags"] \
	[opt [arg flags]]]

Returns a list of the enabled logging options, or reset the list if a
new set of flags is given. Valid flags are: logcombined,
formattedtime, logpartialtimes, logreqtime, checkforproxy, and
suppressquery. They have the same meaning as the similarly named
configuration parameters.


[call [cmd "ns_accesslog maxbackup"] \
	[opt [arg nrfiles]] ]

Sets or gets the number of backup files which will be kept when log rolling is
enabled. If [arg nrfiles] is given it replaces any existing value.



[call [cmd "ns_accesslog maxbuffer"] \
	[opt [arg nrlines]] ]

Sets or gets the maximum number of lines to buffer before being flushed to the
log file.


[call [cmd "ns_accesslog roll"] \
	[opt [arg filepath]]]

Rolls the access log (see [cmd ns_rollfile] for details). Keep [term maxbackup]
old log files around.  If [arg filepath] is given then it overrides the
configuration parameter [term file] as the new log file.



[call [cmd "ns_accesslog rollfmt"] \
	[opt [arg timeformat]] ]

Sets of gets the [arg timeformat] which is appended to the log filename when
rolling. The format string is as understood by the Tcl [cmd clock] command.
If [arg timeformat] is given then it replaces any existing value.


[call [cmd "ns_accesslog scan open"] [opt [arg options]]]

Open an interpreter-local scanner of a regular access-log file. The returned
opaque handle cannot be reconstructed from its string label. The scanner captures
a fixed file-size snapshot; appends are read by opening another scanner from the
last committed checkpoint. Scanning does not flush or otherwise change the logger.
Batch scans require an already loaded, batch-capable nsdb module, but no driver,
pool or connection. Ordinary logging remains independent of nsdb; scanning never
loads a database module implicitly.

[para] Options in this initial producer implementation are:
[list_begin itemized]
[item] [option -filename] [arg path]: defaults to this module's current log.
[item] [option -format] [arg auto|common|combined]: for the current log,
defaults to its logcombined flag; for historical files, defaults to auto.
Explicit auto always enables recognition from the record contents. Automatic
recognition distinguishes an initial five-number timing field from two combined
header fields, but historical quoted extension fields can be ambiguous.
[item] [option -timingcolumn] [arg auto|none|0|2]: for the current log,
defaults to none when logpartialtimes is disabled, otherwise the timing column
for the selected format. Historical files default to auto. Explicit columns count
quoted fields after the byte count, excluding a bare request time. The five
partial times follow producer order: start, accept, queue, filter, run.
Driver-generated phase counters are unavailable worker measurements.
[item] [option -requesttime] [arg auto|true|false]: defaults to the current log's
logreqtime flag, or auto for historical files. True expects a bare numeric request
time following the common/combined fields; false does not consume one.
[item] [option -extendedheaders] [arg auto|headerList]: defaults to the current
log's effective extended-header configuration, or auto for historical files. An
explicit list uses the logger's plain request names or request:/response: tags
(including req:/res: abbreviations). Names are normalized into request-then-response
output order. An empty list declares no extensions; auto leaves their number and
names unknown. A header named auto can be specified as request:auto.
Known trailing header fields are excluded from format/timing recognition and
must be quoted. Their names appear in source metadata under extraheaders;
extendedFields still retains the original lexical text, without adding dynamic
columns or converting header values.
[item] [option -offset] [arg bytePosition]: defaults to zero and must be at a
record boundary. [option -checkpoint] [arg value] resumes validated state instead;
the two options are mutually exclusive.
[item] [option -encoding] [arg utf-8]: only strict UTF-8 is supported initially.
Invalid source bytes produce bounded hexadecimal diagnostic excerpts.
[item] [option -maxlinebytes] [arg n]: defaults to 65536, with supported values
128 through 1048576. Oversized lines are discarded incrementally with a bounded
retained prefix, then emitted as diagnostic records covering their complete span.
[item] [option -tail] [arg pending|final]: defaults to pending. An unterminated
last line remains unaccounted for in pending mode. Final mode emits it for a
completed immutable input file; appending to such a finalized partial line is not
a supported continuation boundary.
[list_end]

[para] Current-file defaults are selected by device/inode identity, including
when -filename names an alias of the current file. Flags and effective header
names are copied when the scanner opens, so later logger changes do not alter an
existing scanner. Explicit scanner options override their respective defaults.
Checkpoints record the effective layout and header names; resuming with different
settings is rejected. Current settings cannot describe older records written
under different configurations in the same file: use explicit auto options or the
known historical layout when scanning such data. Timestamp spelling and the
thread-name placeholder are recognized from each record; query suppression,
masking and proxy handling are already reflected in the logged values.

[call [cmd "ns_accesslog scan next"] [arg scanner] [opt [arg options]]]

Return an opaque, immutable lexical batch backed by compact shared-schema C
storage. Scanning frames and decodes fields but does not convert source numbers
or timestamps to native database types. Original numeric spellings, including
trailing decimal zeros and values beyond native integer ranges, remain available
to a later sink. The sink must enforce schema constraints before insertion.

[para] [option -maxrecords] defaults to 512 and permits 1 through 1000000 records,
including diagnostics. [option -maxbytes] defaults to 8388608 and limits retained
value payload; fixed cell/reference capacity, metadata, source buffers and
allocator overhead are additional. To guarantee progress it must allow at least
four times maxlinebytes plus 2048 bytes. [option -maxscanbytes] defaults to
16777216 and limits examined input bytes. The two byte limits permit positive
values through 1073741824. [option -timeout] defaults to 100ms (cooperative checks,
not a hard deadline); supported values are 1 microsecond through one hour.

[para] Every successful call returns a batch, even when it has zero rows. Metadata
state is ready (a batch limit), budget (scan byte/time limit), snapshot-end
(fully accounted for snapshot) or pending (unfinished final line). Framing state
survives budget calls. Only emitted records advance safe coverage. Metadata and
checkpoints do not commit anything: applications must account for records and
persist the checkpoint atomically in their later database transaction. After a
scan error, reopen from the last successfully accounted-for checkpoint rather
than assuming the failed call delivered its speculative progress.

[call [cmd "ns_accesslog scan info"] [arg scannerOrBatch]]

For a scanner, report source identity, captured snapshot end, consumed read offset,
safe record offset and state. A closed scanner reports closed. Read position can
be ahead of safe position while a partial line is retained; internal file reads
can additionally prefetch snapshot bytes.

[para] For a batch, report batchVersion, representation compact-schema-lexical,
producer, views, schema, rows, payloadBytes, storageBytes, source, coverage, checkpoint,
state and diagnostic counts. The shared schema has ID naviserver.accesslog.scan,
version 1, with ordered JSON base types, refinements, nullability and source
representations. Its columns cover offsets, timestamp text, peer/thread/request
identity, user/request/method/target/queryless path/protocol, status/bytes,
combined headers, request/phase times, source/driver/pool, timing availability,
raw extension fields and parse diagnostics. Timestamp text retains epoch or
calendar syntax; date/time interpretation and range checks belong to the sink.
Unrecognized extension fields remain raw text rather than guessed columns.

[para] storageBytes describes batch/cell/arena capacity, excluding Tcl metadata,
wrappers, the shared schema and allocator bookkeeping; it is not process RSS.
Metadata inspection does not materialize record Tcl objects. Returning or modifying
a Tcl metadata dictionary cannot change the underlying batch.

[para] Checkpoints are versioned Tcl values containing file device/inode, offset,
prefix and boundary anchors, and parser configuration. Resume validates identity,
anchors and configuration. Truncation and supported rotation/replacement cases
are detected; this assumes append-only input between snapshots and does not prove
that an arbitrary in-place rewrite never occurred. A renamed open file remains
readable through the original descriptor. Checkpoints must be saved as complete
Tcl values, including their binary anchor fields.

[call [cmd "ns_accesslog scan triples"] [arg batch] \
    [opt "[option -offset] [arg rowIndex]"] [opt "[option -limit] [arg count]"]]

Materialize JSON triples on demand for selected rows. Defaults are row offset zero
and limit 512; zero limit gives an empty view. The result is a canonical ns_json
root-wrapped array of object triples. Names/type atoms and the schema are shared;
view value objects are created only for the requested rows. Null, empty string
and a literal null string remain distinct. Numbers remain lexical Tcl strings.
The view can be emitted with [cmd "ns_json value"]. Complete eager views can be
expensive and are not a mandatory native-ingestion step.

[para] The batch wrapper and scalar access interface are shared with nsdb. Batch info
and triples access delegate to that interface; the producer's compact C storage
layout remains private and is adopted without copying row values. Use
[cmd "ns_db batchinfo"] and [cmd "ns_db batchinsert"] for backend-independent
inspection and insertion. System-log scanning, nested scalar/container storage
and a schema-associated dictionary import/view are not implemented by this stage.

[call [cmd "ns_accesslog scan close"] [arg scanner]]

Close the descriptor. Repeated close is harmless; next on a closed scanner fails.
Batches already returned remain readable after close. Unreferenced scanner
wrappers close their descriptors automatically, and interpreter deletion closes
all its remaining scanners. Scanner and batch handles are interpreter-local;
cross-thread/interpreter transfer and reconstruction from strings are unsupported.

[para] Scanner-specific errors use the NS_ACCESSLOG SCAN error-code prefix with
OPEN, OPTION, BOUNDARY, CHECKPOINT_MISMATCH, SOURCE_CHANGED, CLOSED, HANDLE, OWNER,
LIMIT or UNAVAILABLE details. Ordinary option parsing may return Tcl argument
errors. Shared batch validation uses NS_DB BATCH error codes. The existing logger
control commands are unaffected.

[list_end]



[section CONFIGURATION]

The nslog module is loaded per-server.

[example_begin]
 ns_section "ns/server/server1/modules" {
   ns_param   nslog   nslog.so
 }
 ns_section "ns/server/server1/module/nslog" {
   ns_param file              access.log
   ns_param logthreadname     true
   ns_param logpartialtimes   true
   ns_param slowlogfile       slow-requests.log
   ns_param slowlogconntime   3s
   ns_param slowlogfiltertime 1s
   ns_param slowlogqueuetime  500ms
 }
[example_end]


The following are valid configuration parameters:

[list_begin definitions]

[def checkforproxy]
If true then the value of the x-forwarded-for HTTP header is logged as the IP
address of the client. Otherwise, the IP address of the directly
connected host is logged. Default: false.

[para] The parameter [def checkforproxy] is deprecated in favor of the
more general reverse proxy mode of the server.

[def driver]
Name of the driver initiating the requests. This option can be used
to produce different access logs for requests submitted via
different drivers to sort out e.g. local server talk.  Per default,
every request is logged. When a value is provided, only requests are
logged in this log file when the request comes from a driver matching
the provided glob pattern.

[def file]
String: path to the log file. Default: access.log

[def extendedheaders]
A space separated list of additional HTTP headers whose values should be logged.
Default: no extra headers are logged.

[def formattedtime]
If true, log the time in common-log-format. Otherwise log seconds since the
epoch. Default: true.

[def logcombined]
If true, log the referrer and user-agent HTTP headers (NCSA combined
format). Default: true.

[def logpartialtimes]
If true then include the high-resolution start time of the request
together with partial request durations (accept, queue, filter,
run) in the access log.
Default: false.

[def logreqtime]
If true then log the total amount of time the request took to run in seconds and
milliseconds.  Default: false.

[def masklogaddr]
Mask IP address in log file for GDPR (similar to the "anonip" IP anonymizer of the
Swiss privacy foundation). When this parameter is set, all IP
addresses are masked in the log file, such that the host-specific (=
person-specific) information is masked out and the IP address does not
match a particular individual anymore.  Still, with the masking turned
on, reverse DNS lookup and/or geolocation is possible. Default: false.

[def maskipv4]
Mask to be used for IPv4 addresses, when [term masklogaddr] is true.
Default: 255.255.255.0

[def maskipv6]
Mask to be used for IPv6 addresses, when [term masklogaddr] is true.
Default: ff:ff:ff:ff::

[def maxbuffer]
The number of log entries to buffer before flushing to the log file. Default: 0.

[def maxbackup]
Number of old log files to keep when log rolling is enabled. Default: 100.

[def rolllog]
If true then the log file will be rolled. Default: true.

[def rollfmt]
Specifies the a suffix for the file when it is rolled (timestamp
based file naming). The format is
provided via the Tcl format codes for timestamp, such, as e.g.,
[const %Y-%m-%d]. Default: ""


[def rollhour]
The hour of the day (0-23) to roll the log file if log rolling is enabled.
Default: 0 (midnight).

[def rollonsignal]
If true then the log file will be rolled when the serve receives a SIGHUP
signal. Default: false.

[def slowlogfile]
Optional additional access log for slow requests. Relative paths are resolved
against the server log directory, as for [const file]. Setting a nonempty
filename enables this log. It must differ from the main access-log filename.
The same formatted record is written to both logs, including all configured
formatting, masking, header, thread-name, and timing options. The slow log is
written without the main log's [const maxbuffer] delay. Both logs rotate on
the same schedule, signal, or [cmd {ns_accesslog roll}], using the same
[const rollfmt] and [const maxbackup]. Default: disabled.

[para]
This brings the long-call monitoring practice from OpenACS to plain NaviServer.
A request is selected when any configured threshold below is exceeded.
Omitted thresholds are disabled; zero is a valid threshold. Selection does
not require [const logpartialtimes]. For analysis and request correlation,
enable [const logpartialtimes] and [const logthreadname]; the connection
thread name includes the pool and request identifier.

[def slowlogconntime]
Time spent in the connection thread: filter time plus runtime, excluding
accept and queue time. Accepts NaviServer time values such as [const 3s].
Default: disabled.

[def slowlogfiltertime]
Filter-time threshold, for example [const 1s]. Default: disabled.

[def slowlogqueuetime]
Queue-time threshold, for example [const 500ms]. Default: disabled.

[def suppressquery]
If true then the query (everything after the ? in the URL) is not logged.
Default: false.

[list_end]




[section EXAMPLES]

The path of the active access log.

[example_begin]
nscp:1> [cmd ns_accesslog] file
/home/ns/servers/server1/modules/nslog/access.log
 
nscp:2> [cmd ns_modulepath] server1 nslog access.log
/home/ns/servers/server1/modules/nslog/access.log
[example_end]



The access log can be rolled manually.

[example_begin]
nscp:1> ns_accesslog roll
[example_end]



Extended logging options can be configured at run-time.

[example_begin]
nscp:1> [cmd ns_accesslog] flags
logCombined
 
nscp:2> [cmd ns_accesslog] flags {logcombined formattedtime}
logcombined formattedtime
 
nscp:3> [cmd ns_accesslog] extendedheaders
 
nscp:4> [cmd ns_accesslog] extendedheaders {Cookie Accept}
Cookie Accept
[example_end]

Example for writing multiple access logs per driver for a single
server: In this example, the loopback traffic is written to a separate
log file.

[example_begin]
 #
 # Configure extra nssock driver for loopback traffic
 #
 ns_section "ns/modules" {
   ns_param loopback  ${bindir}/nssock
 }
 
 ns_section ns/module/loopback {
   ns_param	defaultserver	$server
   ns_param	address		127.0.0.1
   ns_param	port		8888
 }
 
 #
 # Load nslog modules twice with different names
 #
 ns_section ns/server/${server}/modules {
   ns_param	nslog		${bindir}/nslog
   ns_param	nslog2		${bindir}/nslog
 }
 
 #
 # Configure driver patterns (default "*")
 #
 ns_section ns/server/${server}/module/nslog2 {
   ns_param	file	  ${logroot}/access-loopback.log
   ns_param	driver 	  "loopback"
 }
 ns_section ns/server/${server}/module/nslog {
   ns_param	file	  ${logroot}/access.log
   ns_param	driver 	  "ns*"
 }
[example_end]



